speechtext:
The house resumed from Monday, February 18, consideration of the motion of Mr. Kellner:
Whereas the need for establishment of national highways is greater than in 1919;
And whereas in that year the Dominion government recognized its responsibility in the matter by a substantial grant, which grant is now exhausted;
Therefore be it resolved that in the opinion of this house the government should consider the advisibility that a further federal grant be made to cover the next five years' development of highway construction.

It may be that the province of Ontario bears part of the cost, as other provinces do, of roads through the smaller municipalities, but I venture to say that Ontario does not bear any portion of the cost of construction of roads in the large cities;
I should be very much surprised to learn that that was the case. Generally, at all events, the principle I am endeavouring to advance is a principle which has been generally recognized in Canada. Of course, there are exceptions to it just as there must always be exceptions to the general principle as applied to the relations between the provinces and the Dominion.
With respect to the resolution before the house, the discussion on it has scarcely followed the terms of the resolution itself. Several speakers have referred to the necessity of national highways. The resolution itself deals, I take it, with the former scheme of federal aid to highways, which has just recently come to an end. May I point out that there is an important difference between these two things?
